{"records":[{"id":"4mb5kjywkj","title":"Western Blot raw data of the manuscript titled \"In vivo CRISPR knockout screen identifies p47 as a suppressor for HER2+ breast cancer metastasis by regulating NEMO trafficking and autophagy flux\"","description":"The raw images of WB is for the reviewers' request when reviewing the manuscript titled \"In vivo CRISPR knockout screen identifies p47 as a suppressor for HER2+ breast cancer metastasis by regulating NEMO trafficking and autophagy flux\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-12-04T00:26:32.621Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4mb5kjywkj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4mb5kjywkj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mingang","last_name":"Hao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f5jtt3s8hv","title":"GCN2 is a determinant of the response to WEE1 kinase inhibition in small-cell lung cancer ","description":"Patients with small-cell lung cancer (SCLC) are in dire need of more effective therapeutic options. Frequent disruption of the G1 checkpoint in SCLC cells creates a dependency on the G2/M checkpoint to maintain genomic integrity. Indeed, in pre-clinical models, inhibiting the G2/M checkpoint kinase WEE1 shows promise in inhibiting SCLC growth. However, toxicity and acquired resistance have limited the clinical effectiveness of this strategy. Here, using CRISPR/Cas9 knockout screens in vitro and in vivo, we identified multiple factors influencing the response of SCLC cells to the WEE1 kinase inhibitor AZD1775, including the GCN2 kinase and other members of its signaling pathway. Rapid activation of GCN2 upon AZD1775 treatment triggers a stress response in SCLC cells. Pharmacological or genetic activation of the GCN2 pathway enhances cancer cell killing by AZD1775. Thus, activation of the GCN2 pathway represents a novel approach for augmenting the efficacy of WEE1 inhibitors in SCLC.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-06-26T09:21:15.032Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f5jtt3s8hv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f5jtt3s8hv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alexander","last_name":"Drainas","orcid":"0000-0001-9081-9850"},{"first_name":"Julien","last_name":"Sage","orcid":"0000-0002-8928-9968"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Flow Cytometry","Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4g722x4jfg","title":"Western blot","description":"Western blot of CRISPR-Cas9-mediated nontargeting (NT) versus BACH2 knockout 3 days after CRISPR-Cas9-gRNA nucleofection of human gut cells.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-07-02T21:20:55.966Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4g722x4jfg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4g722x4jfg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yulong","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Gut","Western Blot","T Cell","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"65pfcj4j4m","title":"Protocol of CRISPR-Cas9 knockout screens for identifying ferroptosis regulators","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent programmed cell death triggered by excessive lipid peroxidation, has shown promising therapeutic potentials in human diseases. Here, we describe a protocol of a CRISPR-Cas9 loss-of-function screen to identify regulators in response to different inducers of ferroptosis. We emphasize the steps of library amplification, drug treatment, high-throughput sequencing preparation, and bioinformatics analysis using Model-based Analysis of Genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 Knockout (MAGeCK). We also present a method to discover the regulators of ferroptosis and verify the potential targets efficiently.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-24T01:46:48.793Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/65pfcj4j4m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/65pfcj4j4m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wei","last_name":"Gu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9","Ferroptosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9d5499gb8x","title":"In Vivo CRISPR Screens Identify E3 Ligase Cop1 as a Modulator of Macrophage Infiltration and Cancer Immunotherapy Target","description":"Despite remarkable clinical efficacy of immune checkpoint blockade (ICB) in cancer treatment, ICB benefits in triple-negative breast cancer (TNBC) remain limited. Through pooled in vivo CRISPR knockout (KO) screens in syngeneic TNBC mouse models, we found that deletion of the E3 ubiquitin ligase Cop1 in cancer cells decreases the secretion of macrophage-associated chemokines, reduces tumor macrophage infiltration, enhances anti-tumor immunity, and strengthens ICB response. Transcriptomics, epigenomics, and proteomics analyses revealed that Cop1 functions through proteasomal degradation of the C/ebpδ protein. Cop1 substrate Trib2 functions as a scaffold linking Cop1 and C/ebpδ, which leads to polyubiquitination of C/ebpδ. In addition, deletion of the E3 ubiquitin ligase Cop1 in cancer cells stabilizes C/ebpδ to suppress the expression of macrophage chemoattractant genes. Our integrated approach implicates Cop1 as a target for improving cancer immunotherapy efficacy in TNBC by regulating chemokine secretion and macrophage infiltration in the tumor microenvironment.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-08-24T14:43:28.246Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9d5499gb8x","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9d5499gb8x"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"xiaoqing","last_name":"wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9xkgn447vz","title":"In vivo CRISPR Screens Identify E3 Ligase Cop1 as a Modulator of Macrophage Infiltration and Cancer Immunotherapy Target","description":"Summary: Despite remarkable clinical efficacies of immune checkpoint blockade (ICB) in cancer treatment, ICB benefits in triple-negative breast cancer (TNBC) remain limited. Through pooled in vivo CRISPR knockout (KO) screens in syngeneic TNBC mouse models, we found that inhibition of the E3 ubiquitin ligase Cop1 in cancer cells decreases the secretion of macrophage-associated chemokines, reduces tumor macrophage infiltration, enhances tumor immunity and ICB response. Transcriptomics, epigenomics, and proteomics analyses revealed Cop1 functions through proteasomal degradation of the C/ebpδ protein. Cop1 substrate Trib2 functions as a scaffold linking Cop1 and C/ebpδ, which leads to polyubiquitination of C/ebpδ. Cop1 inhibition stabilizes C/ebpδ to suppress the expression of macrophage chemoattractant genes. Our integrated approach implicates Cop1 as a target for improving cancer immunotherapy efficacy by regulating chemokine secretion and macrophage infiltration in the TNBC tumor microenvironment.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-08-23T12:28:59.397Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9xkgn447vz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9xkgn447vz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Collin","last_name":"Tokheim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Binbin","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Protein Degradation","Immunotherapy","Ubiquitylation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3ctpx4k6jp","title":"Porcine reproductive and respiratory syndrome virus upregulates SMPDL3B to promote viral replication by modulating lipid metabolism","description":"We generated a collection of porcine genome-scale CRISPR/Cas9 knockout (PigGeCKO) cells on a PAM background.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-26T18:53:54.241Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3ctpx4k6jp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3ctpx4k6jp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"huanh","last_name":"Shen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Agricultural Biotechnology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"sxrvzkr4hh","title":"Pooled in vitro and in vivo CRISPR-Cas9 screening identifies tumor suppressors in human colon organoids","description":"UMI validation screen, original data from in vivo tumor suppressor CRISPR/Cas9 screening experiment. Figure 6.\nExperimental setup: 281 tumor suppressor gRNAs, positive controls, neutral controls and gRNAs targeting essential genes were introduced into the CRISPR-UMI lentivirus (Michlits et al., 2017). Transduced AK organoids (APC-KO and KRASG12D) were transplanted subcutaneously in NSG mice followed by barcode sequencing of the tumor pool (from 10 mice) and 5 individual tumors. Clone and read number were determined by next generation sequencing. The conventional gRNA enrichment analysis was compared to UMI-CRISPR-screening with outlier removal.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-04-20T15:46:17.868Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/sxrvzkr4hh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/sxrvzkr4hh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Henner","last_name":"Farin","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nknd6w2kbm","title":"Supplementary information's for accepted manuscript in Cell press \"Star protocols\" :A Simple Method to Isolate a Single Human Cell PRDX1 Knockout Generated by CRISPR-Cas9 System","description":"Revised Supplementary information's for accepted manuscript in Cell press \"Star protocols\" :A Simple Method to Isolate a Single Human Cell PRDX1 Knockout Generated by CRISPR-Cas9 System","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-02-09T15:20:06.778Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nknd6w2kbm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nknd6w2kbm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mustapha","last_name":"Aouida","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Life Sciences","Cell Biology","Genetic Engineering","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"48pfmwbcb5","title":"Synergistic effect of capsaicin and docetaxel in prostate cancer cells through AMP-activated kinase","description":"Data used to generate figures of the paper “Combination of the natural product capsaicin and docetaxel synergistically kills human prostate cancer cells through the metabolic regulator AMP-activated kinase” Sánchez BG, Bort A, Mateos-Gómez PA, Rodríguez-Henche N, Díaz-Laviada I. Cancer Cell Int. 2019 Mar 8;19:54. doi: 10.1186/s12935-019-0769-2. eCollection 2019.\nIn this study we demonstrate that the spicy ingredient of peepers, capsaicin, sensitizes prostate cancer cell to docetaxel. The metabolic regulator AMPK plays a pivotal role.\n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-05-29T15:24:03.174Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/48pfmwbcb5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/48pfmwbcb5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ines","last_name":"Diaz-Laviada","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Belen","last_name":"Sanchez","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alicia","last_name":"Bort","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Pedro","last_name":"Mateos","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nieves","last_name":"Rodríguez-Henche","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Prostate Cancer","Docetaxel","Capsaicin","Synergism"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":105841,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":115,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":105841,"collectio